1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:04,020
What if your arms could move...
without you telling them to?

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:04,970
Let's try it.

3
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:05,680
Right now.

4
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,190
Step into a doorway.

5
00:00:07,210 --> 00:00:10,480
Press the backs of your hands
outward against the frame.

6
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:12,130
Firmly, but don't force it.

7
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:14,270
And hold it for 30 seconds.

8
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,100
A neurologist described this in 1915.

9
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,740
It's called the Kohnstamm phenomenon.

10
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,600
More than a hundred years later, scientists
still don't fully agree on why it happens.

11
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:28,760
Right now, your shoulder muscles are
working nonstop against the frame.

12
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:32,360
Hold an effort like that long
enough, and even after you stop...

13
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:36,110
your muscles can keep contracting
on their own for a few seconds.

14
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:38,350
Just a little longer...

15
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,070
Now let go.

16
00:00:41,090 --> 00:00:42,540
Step out of the doorway...

17
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:44,480
and let your arms go loose.

18
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:45,740
Are they rising?

19
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:47,180
That's not you lifting them.

20
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,940
That's your nervous system.

21
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,780
If nothing happened, don't worry.

22
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:51,580
Responses vary.

23
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,830
Some people feel only a
little, or nothing at all.

